<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neolithic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New Stone Age</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neolithic, or New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, is the period in which human communities adopted farming, the herding of domesticated animals and settled village life while still using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>. It began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> around 10,000 BCE and spread over the following millennia into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with independent beginnings in other regions. It brought polished stone axes, pottery in many areas and the first large permanent settlements.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neolithic.jpg" alt="Neolithic" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sarah Murray from Palo Alto, CA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asikli_Hoyuk_sarah_c_murray_6176.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Neolithic, from the Greek for new and stone, was coined by John Lubbock in 1865 as a refinement of the three-age system. The wide-ranging developments of the period, which appear to have arisen independently in several parts of the world, were later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Neolithic Revolution</a>, a term coined in the 1920s by the archaeologist Vere Gordon Childe. In West Asia farming appeared about 12,000 years ago, and there the period lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a>, marked by metallurgy, from about 4500 BCE.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> the Neolithic arose from the Natufian culture, whose people had come to depend on wild cereals and had begun to live in one place. Climatic changes associated with the Younger Dryas are thought to have pushed them toward farming. The founder crops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> were wheat, lentil, pea, chickpea, bitter vetch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The identifying feature of Neolithic technology is the use of polished or ground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, in contrast to the flaked tools of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>. The polished stone axe above all allowed forest to be cleared on a large scale. Farmers made sickle blades and grinding stones, and also pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and statuettes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mud-brick</a> houses coated with plaster began to appear, so that some have called the period the Age of Clay.</p><p>Most Neolithic societies were relatively simple and egalitarian, though more hierarchical than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a> cultures before them. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> states generally arose only with metallurgy. Outside Eurasia, states formed during the local Neolithic in three areas: the Preceramic Andes with the Caral-Supe civilisation, Formative Mesoamerica and ancient Hawaiʻi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Neolithic spread unevenly. Farming appeared in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 7th millennium BCE, and archaeogenetics has shown that its spread to Europe was strongly tied to the migration of early farmers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> about 9,000 years ago. In north-west Europe the Neolithic began much later, from about 4500 BCE to 1700 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it lasted until about 3150 BCE, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until about 2000 BCE.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> the first food production was mobile herding, and archaeologists speak of a Pastoral Neolithic. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> different terms are used, such as the Formative stage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> is generally held not to have had a Neolithic period. The temple of Ġgantija on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> is described as the oldest known freestanding structure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neolithic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens was inhabited continuously from the <b>Neolithic</b>, and in the Late Bronze Age the Acropolis carried a fortified Mycenaean palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The site has been settled since <b>Neolithic</b> times, and it is among the oldest continuously inhabited towns in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was inhabited from the <b>Neolithic</b>, and a Bronze Age settlement at Emporio on its south coast had contacts with the Minoan and Mycenaean worlds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeology shows farming and iron working in the <b>Neolithic</b>, and the aboriginal Dagomba, the Dagbon Sablisi, lived in a decentralised society of towns, Tinsi, each headed by a tindana, an earth priest who pacified the tingbana, the earth gods, the bina and the buga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The site had been settled since the <b>Neolithic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was inhabited in the <b>Neolithic</b> and Bronze Age, and a cult of the goddess Hera at a marshy site near the mouth of the Imbrasos river, later the great sanctuary of the Heraion, reaches back at least to the end of the Bronze Age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
